---
description: Preserve goals, evidence, and exact next actions across planner, implementer, and verifier model switches
alwaysApply: true
---

# Bidirectional Model Handoff

- Roles, not model names, grant authority. Route most bounded, reversible, test-protected work to economical implementers; use capable models for planning/verification and judgment-heavy or high-risk implementation.
- Read `.model-handoff/CONTROL.md` first. On explicit user exit, set `Mode: paused` and retain every protocol file; while paused, skip this contract except safe control/resume handling. Only explicit user resume sets `active`.
- When active, bootstrap a new or compacted context first with `python3 .model-handoff/handoff.py snapshot .`; if the
  helper is unavailable, read `STATUS.md`, `MODEL_HANDOFF.md`, and Git state.
  Expand only the exact plan/report sections named as required context.
- Do not edit until you can state the objective, frozen scope, completed evidence,
  remaining work, stop conditions, and one exact next action.
- The planner/verifier hands off objective, non-goals, allowed files/actions,
  ordered steps, acceptance tests, evidence format, authority boundary, stop
  conditions, and the first exact implementation action.
- Behavioral-defect handbacks state the root invariant, reproducer, adjacent variant, and rejected shallow fixes; non-behavioral corrections may explain why variants are inapplicable.
- Objectives, scope, gates, and invariants are frozen; implementation mechanics
  are not. Inside that boundary, the implementer may refactor locally, replace a
  proposed mechanism, and add adjacent tests when this better satisfies the gate,
  but may not leave allowed paths or add dependencies, public/API/schema changes,
  migrations, or external writes without explicit authority.
- For path, identity, concurrency, snapshot/cache, state-publication, recovery, or security work, cover every applicable value, identity, path, ordering, observation/publication, replacement, failure, and interruption dimension.
- A second post-fix failure of the same invariant stops serial patching: return to planning/verification, mark `high-risk`, and rebuild the coverage matrix before edits.
- The implementer hands back exact changes, files, commands/results, failed
  attempts, deviations, repository/process state, risks, and the precise verification
  or decision requested.
- Obey `Verification mode`; verify acceptance boundaries, not model switches or micro-steps. A stage is the largest cohesive batch sharing scope, invariants, and gates—not a file, test, or plan item—and not every check needs a model switch.
- For eligible capable execution, `self` is one integrated closeout, not a handoff: inspect plan conformance, diff scope, named evidence, and deviations in the current context; do not reload the full snapshot or repeat already evidenced gates.
- Use `independent` in a separate capable context for economical output, high-risk work, material deviations, or required independence. Protocol verification never invokes generic `/review`; only a preselected `bugbot` or `security` mode runs its exact gate. Never ask the user to choose a reviewer.
- The helper `check` validates contract/status/Git/routing consistency only; it is not code verification, test evidence, acceptance, Bugbot, or Security. Run it at handoff, resume, or a final protocol decision—not after each edit or test.
- If a Bugbot/Security selector appears unexpectedly, cancel it and treat “return to the project handoff” as a routing correction: run the snapshot and obey `Verification mode`. If that mode is missing or invalid for `EXECUTION_TO_VERIFY`, repair it to `independent`; never infer a specialized gate.
- Report `self` as no switch required; when switching, record `Recommended capability: economical | capable` so the user never infers the next model tier.
- Update `MODEL_HANDOFF.md` at every role/model switch and verified stage boundary.
  Dynamic state belongs there and in `STATUS.md`, never in this rule.
- Keep the packet compact and delta-first. Point to exact evidence paths/sections;
  do not paste full plans, diffs, logs, or completed history into the packet.
- `STATUS.md` owns the project dashboard; the handoff owns the transfer contract.
  Their active milestone and exact next action must agree verbatim.
- Never say “tests pass” without commands and counts or artifact paths. Never
  report completion while required gates, cleanup, or documentation remain.
- Do not reinterpret goals, lower gates, repeat completed work, overwrite unknown
  changes, or continue through a material conflict among plan, status, Git, and
  observed evidence.
- One role writes at a time; before switching, record owned commands, processes, external runs, and temporary state, leaving a recoverable repository.
- Before switching, run `python3 .model-handoff/handoff.py check .`; follow the playbook for schema, staged context loading, conflicts, and examples.
- Log switch friction in `.model-handoff/FEEDBACK.md`; keep project fixes local. Propose
  generic changes on tested, sanitized branches; independently verify before `main`, never mid-task.
